{
  "image_name": "8de2caec3811bdfddbf0f0cc339c5644.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure explains how satellite laser altimetry measures sea or ice surface height and contrasts traditional full-waveform systems with ICESat-2’s photon-counting approach. On the left, the geometry is shown: a satellite’s reference point is located relative to Earth’s center, and the altimeter sends a laser pulse to a small surface footprint. The measured range, combined with the satellite altitude vector, yields the surface elevation relative to reference surfaces such as the Earth ellipsoid, geoid, mean sea surface, and instantaneous sea surface.\n\nThe middle graphic emphasizes that ICESat-2 uses precise orbit information from GPS and POD to determine the satellite trajectory, then converts photon event range into geodetic height. It also separates different contributors to ocean height, including mean ocean dynamic topography and short-term sea-surface variability from tides, atmospheric pressure effects, and changing topography.\n\nThe right side compares instrument types. Full-waveform altimetry samples broad return signals with larger, widely spaced footprints, whereas photon-counting records discrete photon arrivals from much smaller, denser footprints. The side and plan views suggest photon-counting provides finer spatial sampling and smaller spot size, which can better resolve complex terrain, vegetation, coastlines, and ice edges.",
